WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.682
Pivot tables and crosstabs are useful when we want to compare two categorical dimensions.

00:00:05.682 --> 00:00:11.727
In the first example, pivot_table summarizes amount by channel and status.

00:00:11.727 --> 00:00:16.487
Rows represent channels. Columns represent status values.

00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:19.057
The cells contain summed revenue.

00:00:19.057 --> 00:00:23.962
Fill_value equals zero makes empty combinations easier to interpret.

00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:29.822
Then crosstab counts how many records occur for each channel and status combination.

00:00:29.822 --> 00:00:32.727
Click Run and compare the two tables.

00:00:32.727 --> 00:00:36.667
Think of pivot_table as similar to an Excel pivot table.

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:40.492
It is useful when you want a compact matrix-style summary.

00:00:40.492 --> 00:00:45.512
Crosstab is especially convenient when you want counts of category combinations.

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:51.332
For example, you could compare department by diagnosis, product type by region,

00:00:51.332 --> 00:00:54.347
or survey response by demographic group.

00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:57.452
The important question is what the cell means.

00:00:57.452 --> 00:01:02.232
Is it a count, a sum, an average, or another statistic?

00:01:02.232 --> 00:01:04.793
Always make the meaning of the table clear.
